+# Admin-runbook: pipeline.yml Python-matrix comment honesty (issue #1709)
+
+**Task ID:** `bridge-20261030-ci1709-matrix-honesty-r1` · **Original issue:** #1709 (P2-medium,
+`ci(admin)`, status:unclaimed at claim time) · **Verified against main tip:** `8fead11`
+(2026-10-30 00:40+08 cycle start; re-verified pre-submit) · **Deliverable class:** admin-runbook
+for a `.github/**` protected path — the bridge never auto-applies workflow edits
+(#1643/#1769 doctrine; #1912 + #2169 are the live same-repo precedents).
+
+## 1. Context — the drift, first-hand at main tip
+
+`.github/workflows/pipeline.yml` line 533 comment claims the Python matrix covers
+**(3.10–3.13)** and line 534 adds *"because all four versions are proven here first"* — but the
+only matrix in the file (line 548, job `test-matrix`) runs **`["3.11", "3.12"]`**. Every other
+`python-version:` pin in the file is a fixed `"3.11"` (lines 91/118/142/510/874/964); a whole-file
+scan finds **zero non-comment mentions of 3.10 or 3.13**. The comment is the single stale surface:
+it promises four proven versions and delivers two, so any reader sizing compatibility off the
+comment (the comment's stated purpose — "all four versions are proven here first") is misled.
+Issue #1709 prescribes the remedy direction explicitly: **fix the comment to match the matrix**
+— do not extend the matrix to match the comment (3.10 went EOL 2026-10-01; 3.13 is
+security-only). The signal was filed by radar R24 (PR #1714, docs-only) and the issue has sat
+unclaimed since 2026-10-27 with no PR and no admin response; this runbook packages the
+paste-ready diff so activation is a single admin commit, per the #1912/#2169 pattern.
+
+## 2. Validated patch (ready for admin landing on `.github/workflows/pipeline.yml`, −2/+2 comment lines)
+
+```diff
+--- a/.github/workflows/pipeline.yml
++++ b/.github/workflows/pipeline.yml
+@@ -530,8 +530,8 @@           python3 scripts/ci/frontend_performance_guard.py --check studio/src/App.tsx
+ 
+   # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+-  # 🧪 Python matrix (3.10–3.13) — the ONLY matrix; private CI pins 3.11 after
+-  # the shift-left diet because all four versions are proven here first.
++  # 🧪 Python matrix (3.11–3.12) — the ONLY matrix; private CI pins 3.11 after
++  # the shift-left diet; 3.10 EOL 2026-10-01, 3.13 security-only (#1709, 2026-10-27).
+   # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+   test-matrix:
+     name: Python ${{ matrix.python-version }} Matrix Smoke & Contract Validation
+```
+
+The replacement text preserves the two-line shape of the block it edits (no line-count drift),
+keeps the file's en-dash/emoji style, and embeds the **dated rationale** the issue's acceptance
+criteria demand (EOL date, security-only status, issue ref + decision date) directly in the
+comment — the rationale travels with the code instead of living only in a tracker row.
+
+## 3. Verification evidence (reproduced this cycle — no ghost fixes)
+
+- **V1 — drift is live at tip:** byte-exact read of lines 532–536 at `8fead11`; the stale block is
+  exactly the two quoted lines; `test-matrix:` follows immediately (line 536).
+- **V2 — matrix truth:** `python-version: ["3.11", "3.12"]` is the only matrix literal
+  (line 548); `rg`-level scan shows 3.10/3.13 appear **only** inside the stale comment.
+- **V3 — YAML structural identity:** applied the −2/+2 edit to a copy and compared
+  `yaml.safe_load` parses before/after — **object-identical** (pickle-hash
+  `5ba0da5c385816ec` on both sides; 21 jobs unchanged). Methodology note: `json.dumps(sort_keys=True)`
+  is unusable on this file's parse because PyYAML reads the bare `on:` key as boolean `True`,
+  producing mixed bool/str dict keys — object equality + pickle hashing was used instead. The
+  #1912 quoting footgun (unquoted `#` inside a scalar) **cannot** fire here: the edit is confined
+  to comment lines and never introduces `#` into a scalar.
+- **V4 — acceptance criterion 2 already met:** the interpreter version is exposed in CI logs
+  today via the job name `Python ${{ matrix.python-version }} Matrix Smoke & Contract Validation`
+  (line 537) and the `Set up Python ${{ matrix.python-version }}` step (line 553); no added step
+  is needed, keeping the diff comment-only.
+
+## 4. Alternatives rejected
+
+1. **Extend the matrix to `["3.10", "3.11", "3.12", "3.13"]`** — rejected: the issue prescribes
+   the opposite direction; 3.10 has been EOL since 2026-10-01 (adding it now would prove a dead
+   version and burn runner minutes), and 3.13 is security-only, so "proven here first" would
+   over-claim again — the same honesty defect with four versions instead of two.
+2. **Rewrite the comment without dated rationale** (e.g. just "(3.11–3.12)") — rejected: the
+   issue's acceptance criteria require the matrix decision documented with dated rationale; the
+   chosen text satisfies it in-place and prevents this exact drift class from re-occurring
+   silently (the next editor sees *why* the matrix is two versions).
+3. **Third re-validation receipt for the #1912 runbook instead** — rejected: #2169 already
+   re-validated that diff and the board's dupguard history (#2625/#2655/#2674 a30–a33 wire chain)
+   shows receipt-pairs citing the same parent WO mint spurious wires; #1709 needed first mover,
+   not a fourth receipt.
+4. **Leave for admin discovery** — rejected: three days unclaimed (2026-10-27 → 2026-10-30) with
+   the drift verifiable in one read; the runbook converts the triage into a one-commit handoff.
+
+## 5. Landing order & effect
+
+1. Admin lands the −2/+2 diff (single commit; revert-safe — subtract the two comment lines).
+2. **Zero CI behavior change is structural**: comments are inert to `yaml.safe_load` (V3 proves
+   parse-identity), no step/job/trigger changes, so the landing is green by construction — there
+   is no "day-one red" possibility at all, unlike functional CI edits.
+3. Effect: the CI surface stops over-claiming proven Python versions; compatibility readers get
+   the true supported set plus the dated reason the set is what it is.
+
+## 6. Close semantics (explicit)
+
+Merging the runbook PR closes #1709. Because the packaged diff is comment-only, closure carries
+no behavioral debt: if an admin applies the diff, the repo matches the closed issue immediately;
+if the diff is somehow deferred, the only residue is a stale comment whose correction is fully
+specified above — no mirror plane, watchdog, or nightly re-minting is involved in this lane.
+
